1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,568
How many ways into the locked room should there be?

2
00:00:02,618 --> 00:00:08,976
In Lesson 4, the locked room had only two doors: one for buyers' browsers, and one for systems like ecom.

3
00:00:09,026 --> 00:00:15,816
In Lesson 11, the card frame came through the first door, and in Lesson 7, ecom's calls came through the second.

4
00:00:15,866 --> 00:00:20,520
This lesson builds those two doors, and shows why nothing else listens.

5
00:00:20,570 --> 00:00:23,016
The room has two kinds of visitor.

6
00:00:23,066 --> 00:00:26,856
Buyers' browsers come from the internet, to type a card into the frame.

7
00:00:26,906 --> 00:00:32,664
Services from other products, like ecom, come from their own servers, to ask for a charge.

8
00:00:32,714 --> 00:00:34,656
One big entrance could serve both.

9
00:00:34,706 --> 00:00:39,216
But it would open every visitor's traffic, and see it all in clear text.

10
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:42,648
That entrance would become the most valuable box in the bank.

11
00:00:42,698 --> 00:00:46,296
So the room has two narrow doors, and nothing else listens.

12
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:51,048
Think of a bank: a counter for the public, and a mail slot for partners.

13
00:00:51,098 --> 00:00:53,040
The first door is the counter.

14
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:54,912
It is the payers' door.

15
00:00:54,962 --> 00:00:58,200
A buyer's browser connects to it over an encrypted line.

16
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:02,808
The counter opens that line with a certificate that every browser trusts.

17
00:01:02,858 --> 00:01:04,680
Then it looks at the request.

18
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:10,416
It passes on only the card frame's few requests: show the frame, send a card, send a code.

19
00:01:10,466 --> 00:01:13,536
Every other request gets the answer "not found".

20
00:01:13,586 --> 00:01:18,744
It also limits how fast one buyer can submit cards, at the bank's chosen rate.

21
00:01:18,794 --> 00:01:21,456
It never limits simply loading the page.

22
00:01:21,506 --> 00:01:23,856
The second door is the mail slot.

23
00:01:23,906 --> 00:01:25,728
It is the callers' door.

24
00:01:25,778 --> 00:01:28,176
A caller's connection arrives sealed.

25
00:01:28,226 --> 00:01:31,728
On the outside of the envelope is the name of the service it wants.

26
00:01:31,778 --> 00:01:33,744
The door reads only that name.

27
00:01:33,794 --> 00:01:35,616
It never opens the envelope.

28
00:01:35,666 --> 00:01:39,168
It passes it on, still sealed, to the service behind it.

29
00:01:39,218 --> 00:01:42,816
Passing a connection along unopened is called pass-through.

30
00:01:42,866 --> 00:01:47,664
Only a few names pass: the card vault, the adapters, and the login server.

31
00:01:47,714 --> 00:01:51,264
Any other name, or no name at all, is refused.

32
00:01:51,314 --> 00:01:57,240
The service behind the door then checks the caller itself, with the caller's certificate and its token.

33
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:00,408
So the door never needs a key to the caller's traffic.

34
00:02:00,458 --> 00:02:02,280
Now watch the product.

35
00:02:02,330 --> 00:02:06,696
The card zone has no screen of its own, so watch what each visitor meets.

36
00:02:06,746 --> 00:02:08,688
The buyer opens the payment page.

37
00:02:08,738 --> 00:02:13,224
The browser shows its padlock, because the counter's certificate is trusted.

38
00:02:13,274 --> 00:02:15,984
The frame loads, and the buyer types a card.

39
00:02:16,034 --> 00:02:20,472
Now someone tries a different address on the same door, an admin page, say.

40
00:02:20,522 --> 00:02:22,800
The answer is plain: not found.

41
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:24,912
Nothing behind the door is named.

42
00:02:24,962 --> 00:02:30,696
Now a buyer, or a script, presses "Send code" again and again, very fast.

43
00:02:30,746 --> 00:02:32,568
The counter steps in.

44
00:02:32,618 --> 00:02:36,840
The frame says: too many tries too fast, try again in a minute.

45
00:02:36,890 --> 00:02:39,192
The session is still good after the wait.

46
00:02:39,242 --> 00:02:44,208
On the callers' side, a service with a name that is not on the list knocks on the mail slot.

47
00:02:44,258 --> 00:02:46,512
The connection simply closes.

48
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:48,504
It never reaches the card vault.

49
00:02:48,554 --> 00:02:55,176
A listed name without a valid certificate gets past the slot, and is then refused by the service itself.

50
00:02:55,226 --> 00:02:59,136
On the bank's side, the team watches the card zone in monitoring.

51
00:02:59,186 --> 00:03:02,064
A part that stops answering raises an alert.

52
00:03:02,114 --> 00:03:03,936
What does this protect?

53
00:03:03,986 --> 00:03:09,192
Without narrow doors, one entrance would hold every caller's secrets in clear text.

54
00:03:09,242 --> 00:03:11,592
One break there would expose everything.

55
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:15,672
With two doors, the counter sees only buyers' frame traffic.

56
00:03:15,722 --> 00:03:17,976
The mail slot holds nothing it can read.

57
00:03:18,026 --> 00:03:21,960
And the database, the keys and the logs have no door at all.

58
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:23,904
That protects the cardholders.

59
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:27,264
It also protects the bank's own network around the room.

60
00:03:27,314 --> 00:03:31,608
The bank's firewalls stay simple: two openings, each with one job.

61
00:03:31,658 --> 00:03:33,480
Back to the bank.

62
00:03:33,530 --> 00:03:37,944
A counter that serves the public a few things, and says no to the rest.

63
00:03:37,994 --> 00:03:41,520
A mail slot that reads only the name on the envelope.

64
00:03:41,570 --> 00:03:45,528
No door ever holds every caller's traffic in clear text.

65
00:03:45,578 --> 00:03:48,168
Next: how a caller gets its certificate.
